@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Potato Make is a Scheme library that aims to simplify the task of
maintaining, updating, and regenerating programs.  It is inspired by
the @command{make} utility in IEEE Std 1003.1-2017 (POSIX).  With this
library, you can write a build script in Guile Scheme.

Like POSIX @command{make}, these scripts update files that are derived
from other files.  A build script typically describes how a file is
built from shell commands, and it describes the relationships between
the components to be built, so that they are built in order.  The
script checks the prerequisites of a target, and if the prerequisites
are newer than the target, it rebuilds the target.

There are two types of rules that a build script can contain.

@enumerate
@item
Target rules, which describe how a specific named file is to be built
from prerequisites using a set of shell commands.
@item
Suffix rules, which generically describe how to convert files with
one file name suffix into files with another file name suffix.
@end enumerate

A build script makes use of a custom variable type which can be set
either in the script, by environment variables, or in command-line
arguments.  They are called @dfn{makevars}, to reduce confusion with
standard Scheme variables.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Potato Make requires GNU Guile, version 2.2 or 3.0.  It is built with
the usual GNU build system commands.

@example
./configure
make
make check
make install
@end example

When building from a checkout of the Git repository rather than from
a release tarball, first create the @file{configure} script by running
@command{autoreconf -vif}.  This requires Autoconf, Automake, Texinfo,
and Guile's development files.

The Scheme modules are installed beneath the installation prefix, in
@file{@var{prefix}/share/guile/site/@var{version}}, and the compiled
modules in @file{@var{prefix}/lib/guile/@var{version}/site-ccache}.
Guile only searches the directories beneath its own prefix, so if
Guile is installed in @file{/usr}, configure Potato Make to match.

@example
./configure --prefix=/usr
@end example

Otherwise, add the directories to the @env{GUILE_LOAD_PATH} and
@env{GUILE_LOAD_COMPILED_PATH} environment variables.

Potato Make can also be used without installing it.  The
@file{pre-inst-env} script in the build directory runs a command with
the uninstalled modules on Guile's load path.

@example
./pre-inst-env guile -s examples/hello.scm
@end example

@node Writing a Build Script
@chapter Writing a Build Script

To write a build script with this library, add the following
boilerplate code at the top of an executable Scheme script.
Throughout this manual the script is presumed to be named
@file{makefile.scm}; however, you may choose any name.

@example
#!/usr/bin/env sh
exec guile -s "$0" "$@@"
!#

(use-modules (potato make))
(initialize)
@end example

Rules go between @code{initialize} and @code{execute}.  Add this at
the bottom of the script:

@example
(execute)
@end example

@code{initialize} parses arguments and prepares the build context.
It reads the process command line and applies options, makevar
assignments (@samp{KEY=VALUE}), and target names.  @code{execute}
then builds the selected targets.

Here is a complete, simple example.

@example
#!/usr/bin/env sh
exec guile -s "$0" "$@@"
!#

(use-modules (potato make))
(initialize)
(:= CC "gcc")
(:= CFLAGS "-g -O2")

(: "all" '("foo"))
(: "foo" '("foo.o" "bar.o")
  (~ ($ CC) "-o" $@@ $^))
(-> ".c" ".o"
  (~ ($ CC) "-c" $<))
(execute)
@end example

@deffn Procedure initialize [arguments]
Set up the options, rules, and makevars.  Any rules and makevars from
an earlier call are discarded, and every option returns to its
default.

If @var{arguments} is not given, the options, makevars, and targets
come from the command line of the process.  Otherwise it is a list of
strings to use in place of the command line.  The first string is the
name of the script, and then any combination of options, makevar
assignments and targets may follow.

@example
(initialize '("makefile.scm" "--verbosity=3" "CC=gcc" "all"))
@end example

If @var{arguments} is the empty list, the program name is taken from
@code{(program-arguments)}, and there are no options, makevars, or
targets.

@example
;; ignore all command line arguments except the script name
(initialize '())
@end example

The return value is the list of targets named in the arguments.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure execute [#:targets targets]
Build targets.  @var{targets}, if provided, is a list of the names of
the targets to build.  Otherwise, the targets named in the arguments
to @code{initialize} are built.  If there were none, the first target
rule in the script is built.

The return value is @code{#t} if every target was built
successfully, and @code{#f} otherwise.  To have the exit status of
the script report whether the build succeeded, end the script with

@example
(exit (execute))
@end example
@end deffn

@node Command-Line Arguments
@chapter Command-Line Arguments

A script that calls @code{initialize} accepts a command line of this
form.

@example
makefile.scm [-hvVeEbknAS] [var=value @dots{}] [target_name @dots{}]
@end example

@table @asis
@item @option{-h}, @option{--help}
Display help and exit.

@item @option{-v}, @option{--version}
Display the version and exit.

@item @option{-V @var{level}}, @option{--verbosity=@var{level}}
Choose the verbosity of the output: 0 is silent, 1 is terse, 2 is
the default, and 3 is verbose.

@item @option{-e}, @option{--environment}
Import environment variables as makevars.

@item @option{-E}, @option{--elevate-environment}
Import environment variables as makevars, and let them, along with
makevars from the command line, take precedence over script
assignments.

@item @option{-b}, @option{--builtins}
Add the built-in makevars and suffix rules.  @xref{Built-in Rules and
Makevars}.

@item @option{--ignore-errors}
Ignore recipe failures and continue.

@item @option{-k}, @option{--continue-on-error}
Continue building later top-level targets after a failure.

@item @option{-n}, @option{--no-execution}
Skip normal recipe execution; run only the recipes marked
@code{always-execute}.  The commands that were skipped are printed.

@item @option{-A}, @option{--ascii}
Use ASCII-only output and no colors.

@item @option{-S}, @option{--strict}
Raise an error when an unset makevar is referenced.

@item @var{var}=@var{value}
Set a makevar from the command line.  The assignment is split at the
first equals sign, so the value may itself contain equals signs, as in
@samp{CFLAGS=-DDEBUG=1}.

@item @var{target_name}
Name a target to build.  If no target is named, the first target rule
defined in the script is used.
@end table

Note that POSIX @command{make}, by default, adds in environment
variables and built-in rules.  With this library, these must be
requested with command-line arguments.  This makes the tool more
appropriate for generating @emph{reproducible builds}.

Some examples:

@example
./makefile.scm
./makefile.scm all
./makefile.scm CC=clang CFLAGS='-O3 -g' all
./makefile.scm --environment --verbosity=3 test
./makefile.scm --continue-on-error bad-target good-target
./makefile.scm --no-execution all
@end example

@node Environment Variables
@chapter Environment Variables

Certain environment variables affect the execution of a build script.

@table @env
@item LANG
Affects the current locale.

@item MAKEFLAGS
When @option{--environment} or @option{--elevate-environment} is
enabled, @env{MAKEFLAGS} is parsed as space-separated tokens.  The
following word tokens are recognized, and have the same effect as the
corresponding command-line options: @samp{silent}, @samp{terse},
@samp{verbose}, @samp{builtins}, @samp{ascii}, @samp{ignore-errors},
@samp{continue-on-error}, @samp{strict}, and @samp{no-execution}.
Options on the command line take precedence.  Tokens of the form
@samp{VAR=VALUE} are imported as makevars, except for @samp{SHELL}
and @samp{MAKEFLAGS} themselves.

@item SHELL
The @env{SHELL} environment variable is always ignored and never
imported as a makevar.
@end table

All other environment variables, including those with null values,
initialize makevars when @option{--environment} or
@option{--elevate-environment} is active.

@node Rules
@chapter Rules

The purpose of a build script is to run rules, which describe how
programs act on prerequisites to create targets.  There are two types
of rules: target rules and suffix rules.

@menu
* Target Rules::                Building one named target.
* Suffix Rules::                Building by file name suffix.
* Recipes::                     The commands of a rule.
@end menu

@node Target Rules
@section Target Rules

@deffn Procedure target-rule name [prerequisites] recipe @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure : name [prerequisites] recipe @dots{}
Add a target rule to the list of target rules.

@var{name} is a string that names the target.  If this rule is being
used to create a file, @var{name} is the name of the file to be
output.

@var{prerequisites}, if provided, is a list of strings.  Each is the
name of a target that must be built, or a file that must exist, before
this target is attempted.  It may be the empty list, indicating that
there are no prerequisites.

The @var{recipe}s are executed in order to create the target.
@xref{Recipes}.  If there is no recipe at all, the rule always passes
once its prerequisites have been built.

If more than one target rule is defined for the same name, the one
defined last is used.
@end deffn

Here are some example target rules.  A recipe as a string to be
executed by the shell:

@example
(: "foo.o" '("foo.c")
  "cc -c foo.c")
@end example

A recipe as a procedure:

@example
(: "clean-foo" '()
  (lambda ()
    (delete-file "foo.o")))
@end example

A recipe as a procedure that returns @code{#f} to indicate failure:

@example
(: "recent" '()
  (lambda ()
    (if condition #t #f)))
@end example

A recipe as a procedure returning a string to be executed by the
shell:

@example
(: "foo.o" '("foo.c")
  (lambda ()
    (format #f "cc ~A -c foo.c" some-flags)))
@end example

A recipe using the recipe helpers, makevars, and automatic variables,
which create a string to be executed by the shell:

@example
(: "foo.o" '("foo.c" "foo.h")
  (~ ($ CC) ($ CFLAGS) "-c" "-o" $@@ $<))
@end example

A recipe as a boolean, to indicate success or failure without doing
any processing.  For example, the rule below tells Potato Make that
the file @file{foo.c} exists without actually testing for it.

@example
(: "foo.c" '() #t)
@end example

A rule with no recipe at all is shorthand for the recipe @code{#t}.
This is used in prerequisite-only target rules:

@example
(: "all" '("foo.exe"))
(: "all" '("foo.exe") #t)   ;; equivalent
@end example

@node Suffix Rules
@section Suffix Rules

Unlike target rules, which are for one specific target and may have
many prerequisites, suffix rules describe how to create a target from
a single prerequisite, with the assumption that they have the same
base name and differ only in their file name suffixes.  They are
applied to prerequisites, and to top-level targets, that have no
target rule of their own.

For example, one could have a suffix rule to convert a @file{.c} file
into a @file{.o} file.

@deffn Procedure suffix-rule source-suffix target-suffix recipe @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure -> source-suffix target-suffix recipe @dots{}
Add a suffix rule to the list of suffix rules.

@var{source-suffix} is a string that is the file name suffix of the
file used to create the target.  Commonly, this string begins with a
period.

@var{target-suffix} is a string that is the file name suffix of the
file to be created.  It may be the empty string, indicating that the
target is just the base name with no suffix.

The @var{recipe}s are executed in order to create the target.
@xref{Recipes}.

A suffix rule whose source and target suffixes are identical is
ignored, with a warning.
@end deffn

A suffix rule applies to a target only if the corresponding source
file exists, or can itself be made by some other rule.  So with rules
for both @samp{.y} to @samp{.c} and @samp{.c} to @samp{.o}, the
target @file{foo.o} can be built from a hand-written @file{foo.c}, or
from a @file{foo.y} by way of a generated @file{foo.c}.  If several
suffix rules apply to a target, they are tried in turn until one
succeeds.

Example suffix rules:

@example
(-> ".c" ".o"
    (~ ($ CC) ($ CFLAGS) "-c" "-o" $@@ $<))

(-> ".sh" ""
    (~ "cp" $< $@@)
    (~ "chmod a+x" $@@))
@end example

@defvar %suffix-rules
The list of suffix rules that have been defined in the build script,
in an internal format.
@end defvar

@node Recipes
@section Recipes

A recipe, in a target rule or in a suffix rule, can be a string, a
procedure, a boolean, or a pair produced by one of the recipe helpers.

If the recipe is a string, it is passed to the @code{system} procedure
for execution by the shell.  If the command exits with a non-zero
status, the recipe has failed.

If the recipe is a procedure, it is called with no arguments.  If it
returns @code{#f} or a non-zero integer, the recipe has failed.  If
it returns a string, that string is passed to @code{system} and is
processed as above.  Any other return value indicates success.

If the recipe is @code{#t}, it always passes without executing
anything.  If the recipe is @code{#f}, it always fails.

If the recipe is a pair, as returned by one of the recipe helpers, the
@sc{car} is one of the symbols @code{default}, @code{ignore-error},
@code{continue-on-error}, @code{silent}, or @code{always-execute},
and the @sc{cdr} is a string or procedure as above.  The symbol
modifies how the recipe is run.  @xref{Recipe Helpers}.

When a recipe fails, the remaining recipes of the rule are not run
and the target has failed, unless errors are being ignored.

@node Recipe Helpers
@chapter Recipe Helpers

The recipe helpers build the command line of a recipe by concatenating
elements, inserting spaces between them.

@example
(~ "string" (lambda () "string") ($ KEY) $@@ 100)
@end example

Elements can be

@itemize
@item
strings,
@item
procedures of no arguments that return strings, such as the automatic
variables,
@item
makevar references,
@item
or anything whose representation as created by @code{display} makes
sense.
@end itemize

Any procedures are called lazily, when the rule is executed.  Empty
strings are skipped, so they don't introduce extra spaces.

Each helper returns a pair whose @sc{car} is a symbol tag and whose
@sc{cdr} is a procedure that produces the composed string.

@deffn Procedure string-compose element @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure ~ element @dots{}
Compose a recipe with the tag @code{default}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure ignore-error-compose element @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure ~- element @dots{}
Like @code{~}, but with the tag @code{ignore-error}.  If the command
fails, the failure is ignored and the rule continues with its next
recipe.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure continue-on-error-compose element @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure ~k element @dots{}
Like @code{~}, but with the tag @code{continue-on-error}.  After a
failure, the rule continues with its next recipe instead of stopping.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure silent-compose element @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure ~@@ element @dots{}
Like @code{~}, but with the tag @code{silent}.  The command is not
printed before it is run, except with verbose output.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure always-execute-compose element @dots{}
@deffnx Procedure ~+ element @dots{}
Like @code{~}, but with the tag @code{always-execute}.  The command
is run even if the @option{--no-execution} option was chosen.
@end deffn

@node Automatic Variables
@chapter Automatic Variables

Recipes can use the following automatic variables.  They are
procedures of no arguments, whose values are set before the recipes
of a rule are run.

@table @code
@item $@@
@itemx target-name
The name of the target.

@item $*
@itemx target-basename
The name of the target with its file name suffix removed.  In a
suffix rule, it is the suffix of the rule that is removed.

@item $<
@itemx primary-prerequisite
The first prerequisite.

@item $^
The prerequisites, as a single space-separated string.

@item $$^
@itemx prerequisites
The prerequisites, as a Scheme list of strings.

@item $?
The prerequisites that are files newer than the target file, as a
single space-separated string.  If the target file doesn't exist, it
is all of the prerequisites.

@item $$?
@itemx newer-prerequisites
The same, as a Scheme list of strings.
@end table

Since they are procedures, call them to get their values in Scheme
code, as in @code{($@@)}.  The recipe helpers call them for you.

@example
(: "archive.tar" '("a.txt" "b.txt")
   (~ "tar cf" $@@ $^)
   (lambda ()
     (format #t "archived ~a files~%" (length ($$^)))))
@end example

These are global variables, and are not thread safe.

@node Makevars
@chapter Makevars

Build scripts may take advantage of a special variable type called a
makevar.  In Scheme terms, makevars are entries in a hash table that
have special accessor syntax.

@itemize
@item
The makevar names --- the keys --- are strings.
@item
The makevar values are strings, or procedures of no arguments that
return strings.
@end itemize

There are five sources for makevars, listed from highest to lowest
priority.

@enumerate
@item
Script assignments (@code{:=}, @code{?=})
@item
Command-line assignments (@samp{KEY=VALUE})
@item
@env{MAKEFLAGS} assignments (when @option{--environment} or
@option{--elevate-environment} is enabled)
@item
Environment variables (when @option{--environment} or
@option{--elevate-environment} is enabled)
@item
Built-ins (when @option{--builtins} is enabled)
@end enumerate

A value from a higher-priority source replaces a value from a
lower-priority source.

This priority is modified by the @option{--elevate-environment}
option.  When it is set, script assignments @emph{do not} override
values from the command line, @env{MAKEFLAGS}, or the environment.
They @emph{do} still override built-ins.

Some notes:

@itemize
@item
@env{SHELL} and @env{MAKEFLAGS} are never imported as makevars.
@item
In strict mode (@option{--strict}), referencing an unset makevar
raises an error.
@item
Without strict mode, an unset makevar is the empty string.  This is
because it is a common practice in makefiles to use variables that
may or may not be defined by the environment.
@end itemize

The library provides the following procedures and syntax for makevars.

@deffn Procedure assign key [val]
Set a makevar.  @var{key} is a string, or a procedure of no arguments
that returns a string.  Likewise @var{val}.  If either is a procedure,
it is called immediately.  If @var{val} is not given, the empty
string is used.  A @var{val} that is neither a string nor a procedure
is converted to a string as if by @code{display}.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax := key [val]
A syntax version of @code{assign}, where @var{key} is an unquoted
symbol.

@example
(:= CC "gcc")  @equiv{}  (assign "CC" "gcc")
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure lazy-assign key [val]
Like @code{assign}, except that if @var{val} is a procedure, it is
not called until the first time the makevar is referenced.  Its
result then becomes the value of the makevar.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax ?= key [val]
A syntax version of @code{lazy-assign}, where @var{key} is an
unquoted symbol.

@example
(?= DATE (lambda () (strftime "%F" (localtime (current-time)))))
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure reference key quoted? [transformer]
Look up the makevar @var{key} and return its value as a string.
@var{key} is a string, or a procedure of no arguments that returns a
string.

If the makevar is not set, the empty string is returned, or an error
is raised in strict mode.

If @var{quoted?} is true, the value is returned wrapped in double
quotes.

The optional @var{transformer} is a procedure that takes a string and
returns a string.  It is applied to every whitespace-separated word
in the value, and the results are joined with spaces.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax $ key [transformer]
A syntax version of @code{reference}, where @var{key} is an unquoted
symbol.

@example
($ CC)  @equiv{}  (reference "CC" #f)
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax Q key [transformer]
The same as @code{$}, but the value is wrapped in double quotes.

@example
(Q CC)  @equiv{}  (reference "CC" #t)
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure reference-func key
Return a procedure of no arguments that, when called, looks up the
makevar @var{key} as @code{reference} does.  If the makevar is not
set, return @code{#f}.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax $$ key
A syntax version of @code{reference-func}, where @var{key} is an
unquoted symbol.

@example
($$ CC)  @equiv{}  (reference-func "CC")
@end example
@end deffn

Note that @code{($ CC)} is evaluated where it appears.  In a recipe
helper, that is when the rule is defined, so a makevar should be
assigned before the rules that use it.  To look a makevar up only
when the recipe runs, use a procedure, such as the one returned by
@code{($$ CC)}.

@defvar %makevars
The hash table of makevars, in the @code{(potato makevars)} module.
You are not meant to access it directly, but with the procedures
above.  Each value is a pair: the @sc{car} is a string or a promise,
and the @sc{cdr} is the priority level, an integer.
@end defvar

@node The Build Algorithm
@chapter The Build Algorithm

The top-level targets are given on the command line.  If none are,
the first target rule in the script is used.

For each top-level target, a tree of prerequisites is created.  If a
target has a target rule, its children are the prerequisites of that
rule.  Otherwise, if its name ends with the target suffix of one or
more suffix rules, its children are the files that would fulfill those
suffix rules.  This continues until the tree is populated.

Then the modification times are found for all of the targets that
exist as files.

A node is considered up to date if it has a modification time, it has
children, and none of its children are newer than it is.  Up-to-date
nodes pass without running their recipes.  A target with no
prerequisites is never up to date, so its recipes are always run.

In a depth-first traversal, each node is built:

@itemize
@item
For a @strong{target rule}, all of the children must pass, in order,
before the recipes of the rule are run.
@item
For @strong{suffix rules}, the recipes of a rule are run as soon as
its child passes, trying each suffix rule in turn.
@item
A node with no rule at all passes if it is a file that exists.
@end itemize

If a recipe fails:

@itemize
@item
With @option{--ignore-errors}, the failure is ignored and the build
continues.
@item
With @option{--continue-on-error}, the top-level target has failed,
and the build proceeds to the next top-level target.
@item
Otherwise, the build stops.
@end itemize

@node Built-in Rules and Makevars
@chapter Built-in Rules and Makevars

If the @option{--builtins} option is given, the following makevars
and suffix rules are present by default.

The built-in makevars:

@example
MAKE=make
AR=ar
ARFLAGS=-rv
YACC=yacc
YFLAGS=
LEX=lex
LFLAGS=
LDFLAGS=
CC=cc
CFLAGS=-g -O2
FC=gfortran
FFLAGS=-O1
GUILE=guile
GUILD=guild
GFLAGS=-W2
@end example

The built-in suffix rules are equivalent to the following.  They
look up their makevars when they are run, so they honor assignments
made anywhere in the script.

@example
(-> ".c" ".o"
    (~ ($ CC) ($ CFLAGS) "-c" $<))

(-> ".f" ".o"
    (~ ($ FC) ($ FFLAGS) "-c" $<))

(-> ".y" ".o"
    (~ ($ YACC) ($ YFLAGS) $<))

(-> ".l" ".o"
    (~ ($ LEX) ($ LFLAGS) $<)
    (~ ($ CC) ($ CFLAGS) "-c lex.yy.c")
    "rm -f lex.yy.c"
    (~ "mv lex.yy.o" $@@))

(-> ".y" ".c"
    (~ ($ YACC) ($ YFLAGS) $<)
    (~ "mv y.tab.c" $@@))

(-> ".l" ".c"
    (~ ($ LEX) ($ LFLAGS) $<)
    (~ "mv lex.yy.c" $@@))

(-> ".scm" ".go"
    (~ ($ GUILD) "compile" ($ GFLAGS) $<))
@end example

@node Extensions
@chapter Extensions

Potato Make supports module-based extensions that can decorate
command-line parsing and initialization.  An extension can contribute

@enumerate
@item
additional @code{getopt-long} option entries,
@item
additional lines in the @option{--help} output,
@item
and an initialization hook that receives the parsed options and can
update makevars or rules.
@end enumerate

@deffn Procedure register-extension! #:id id [#:option-spec spec] [#:help-lines lines] [#:init-hook hook]
Register an extension.  @var{id} identifies the extension;
registering an @var{id} a second time has no effect.

@var{spec} is a list of option specifications in the format used by
the @code{(ice-9 getopt-long)} module.  @var{lines} is a list of
strings to be appended to the @option{--help} output.  @var{hook} is
a procedure of one argument, the parsed options, as returned by
@code{getopt-long}.  It is called by @code{initialize}, after the
makevars have been set up.
@end deffn

@example
(register-extension!
  #:id 'my-extension
  #:option-spec '((my-flag (value #t)))
  #:help-lines '("    --my-flag=VALUE"
                 "        example extension option")
  #:init-hook (lambda (options)
                ;; inspect options and set makevars/rules
                #t))
@end example

An extension must be registered before @code{initialize} is called.
If the extension is a module, import it first.

@example
(use-modules (potato make)
             (my extension))
(initialize)
@end example

@menu
* Configure Extension::         GNU-style installation directories.
@end menu

@node Configure Extension
@section Configure Extension

The @code{(potato configure)} module is an extension that comes with
Potato Make.  Importing it before calling @code{initialize} adds the
GNU-style installation directory options, and applies them to makevars
during initialization.

@example
(use-modules (potato make)
             (potato configure))

(initialize)
;; rules
(execute)
@end example

The options from this extension are

@itemize
@item
@option{--prefix=@var{dir}}, @option{--exec-prefix=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--bindir=@var{dir}}, @option{--sbindir=@var{dir}},
@option{--libexecdir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--datarootdir=@var{dir}}, @option{--datadir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--sysconfdir=@var{dir}}, @option{--sharedstatedir=@var{dir}},
@option{--localstatedir=@var{dir}}, @option{--runstatedir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--includedir=@var{dir}}, @option{--oldincludedir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--docdir=@var{dir}}, @option{--infodir=@var{dir}},
@option{--htmldir=@var{dir}}, @option{--dvidir=@var{dir}},
@option{--pdfdir=@var{dir}}, @option{--psdir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--libdir=@var{dir}}, @option{--lispdir=@var{dir}},
@option{--localedir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--mandir=@var{dir}}, @option{--man1dir=@var{dir}},
@option{--man2dir=@var{dir}}
@item
@option{--srcdir=@var{dir}}
@end itemize

For example:

@example
./makefile.scm --prefix=/opt/pkg --exec-prefix=/opt/pkg/exec all
@end example

After initialization, the directory makevars --- @code{prefix},
@code{exec_prefix}, @code{bindir}, @code{mandir}, and so on --- are
set to the defaults from the GNU coding standards, unless overridden
by one of the options above.

@deffn Procedure add-directory-variables [#:prefix prefix] @dots{} [#:package-name name]
Set the directory makevars.  There is a keyword argument for each of
the options above, which overrides the default for that directory.
@var{name} is the name of the package, which is used in the default
value of @code{docdir}.
@end deffn

@node Text and File Name Functions
@chapter Text and File Name Functions

The @code{(potato lib)} module provides procedures for manipulating
lists of words and file names, modeled on the functions of GNU
@command{make}.

@example
(use-modules (potato make)
             (potato lib))
@end example

Most of these procedures operate on a string that holds a list of
whitespace-separated words, and return a string in which the words
are separated by single spaces.

Text within single or double quotes is a single word, even if it
contains whitespace.  The quote characters themselves are removed.
A quote character of one kind inside quotes of the other kind is an
ordinary character.  This makes it possible to work with file names
that contain spaces.

@example
(words "main.c 'alpha bravo.c'")  @result{}  ("main.c" "alpha bravo.c")
@end example

@menu
* Text Functions::
* Word Functions::
* File Name Functions::
@end menu

@node Text Functions
@section Text Functions

@deffn Procedure words text
Split @var{text} into a list of words.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax W key
The value of the makevar @var{key}, an unquoted symbol, as a list of
words.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure strip text
Remove leading and trailing whitespace from @var{text}, and replace
each internal run of whitespace with a single space.  Whitespace
within quotes is preserved.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax S key
The value of the makevar @var{key}, an unquoted symbol, stripped.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure strip-quotes text
Remove every single-quote and double-quote character from @var{text}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure findstring find in
Return @var{find} if it occurs in the string @var{in}.  Otherwise
return the empty string.
@end deffn

@deffn Syntax F find-key in-key
Like @code{findstring}, where both arguments are the names of
makevars, as unquoted symbols.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure subst from to text
Replace every occurrence of the string @var{from} in @var{text} with
the string @var{to}.

@example
(subst "ee" "EE" "feet on the street")  @result{}  "fEEt on the strEEt"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure patsubst pattern replacement text
Replace the words in @var{text} that match @var{pattern} with
@var{replacement}.  @var{pattern} may contain one @samp{%}, which
matches any number of characters.  A @samp{%} in @var{replacement} is
replaced by the text that matched.

@example
(patsubst "%.c" "%.o" "foo.c bar.c baz.h")  @result{}  "foo.o bar.o baz.h"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure filter-in patterns text
Return the words in @var{text} that match any of the words in
@var{patterns}, each of which may contain a @samp{%} wildcard.

@example
(filter-in "%.c %.h" "foo.c bar.s baz.h")  @result{}  "foo.c baz.h"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure filter-out patterns text
Return the words in @var{text} that do @emph{not} match any of the
words in @var{patterns}.
@end deffn

@node Word Functions
@section Word Functions

@deffn Procedure word n text
Return the @var{n}th word of @var{text}, counting from one, or the
empty string if there is no such word.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure firstword text
@deffnx Procedure lastword text
Return the first or the last word of @var{text}, or the empty string
if @var{text} has no words.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure wordlist start end text
Return the words of @var{text} from position @var{start} through
position @var{end}, inclusive, counting from one.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure sort-list text
Sort the words of @var{text} and remove any duplicates.

This replaces Guile's own @code{sort-list} procedure in a module that
imports @code{(potato lib)}.
@end deffn

@node File Name Functions
@section File Name Functions

@deffn Procedure file-dir text
Return the directory part of each file name in @var{text}, including
the trailing slash.  A name with no directory part gives @samp{./}.

@example
(file-dir "src/foo.c hacks")  @result{}  "src/ ./"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure file-notdir text
Return each file name in @var{text} without its directory part.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure file-suffix text
Return the suffix of each file name in @var{text}, including the
period.  A name with no suffix gives the empty string.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure file-basename text
Return each file name in @var{text} without its suffix.

@example
(file-basename "src/foo.c hacks")  @result{}  "src/foo hacks"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure addprefix prefix text
@deffnx Procedure addsuffix suffix text
Add @var{prefix} to the front, or @var{suffix} to the end, of each
word in @var{text}.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure join-two list1 list2
Concatenate the words of @var{list1} and @var{list2} pairwise.  If
one list is longer, its extra words are included unchanged.

@example
(join-two "a b" ".c .o")  @result{}  "a.c b.o"
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure wildcard text
Expand each of the file name patterns in @var{text}, and return the
names of the files that exist.  In a pattern, @samp{*} matches any
number of characters and @samp{?} matches one character.  Wildcards
are only recognized in the last component of a pattern.  As in the
shell, a file whose name begins with a period is matched only by a
pattern that begins with a period.

The matches of each pattern are sorted.

@example
(wildcard "*.c src/*.c")
@end example
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure file-realpath text
Return the canonical absolute name of each file in @var{text},
resolving symbolic links.  Files that don't exist are omitted.
@end deffn

@deffn Procedure file-abspath text
Return the absolute name of each file in @var{text}, without
resolving symbolic links.  The @file{.} and @file{..} components are
resolved lexically.  The files need not exist.
@end deffn

@node Testing Build Scripts
@chapter Testing Build Scripts

@deffn Procedure install-alternate-system-driver proc
Use @var{proc}, in place of Guile's @code{system} procedure, to run
the command strings of recipes.  @var{proc} is called with the
command string, and returns an exit status, where zero indicates
success.  The return value is the driver that was previously
installed.

This is useful for testing a build script, or for running commands in
some custom way.
@end deffn

@example
(define commands '())

(install-alternate-system-driver
 (lambda (command)
   (set! commands (cons command commands))
   0))
@end example

